    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Corbin Carroll** (79.02) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.90) stand as our elite offensive pillars. **Corbin Carroll** gains significant leverage from his batting order slot and a favorable matchup against Holmes, though we are monitoring his stolen base opportunities, as the opposing catcher\u2019s elite hold metrics slightly suppress his overall efficiency. Meanwhile, **Tyler Soderstrom** benefits from a massive park factor boost (+16%), making him our highest-projected hitter outside of the speed-dependent categories. **Josh Naylor** (71.44) also earns a strong start, primarily driven by a high-upside matchup against Kay; the favorable wind conditions at the venue provide the necessary tailwind for his power-heavy profile to shine despite the left-on-left dynamic.\n\n**Mickey Moniak** (66.49) and **James Wood** (63.92) provide excellent secondary value, particularly with **James Wood** batting leadoff against Junk, which maximizes his exposure to run-scoring opportunities. We are also monitoring **Vinnie Pasquantino** (55.60); while his raw score is hampered by the high-skill level of Smith, his platoon advantage provides enough cushion to keep him in the utility spot. The most difficult decision on the bench was **Austin Riley** (49.30), who missed the starting cut due to Snell's ability to suppress power from the right side. While **Austin Riley** carries significant name value, the higher floor provided by our middle-infield starters against inferior arms like Kay and Schlittler ultimately forced him to the pine to preserve our game caps.\n\nRisks today are concentrated in our catchers and bottom-of-the-order bats. **Gabriel Moreno** (56.80) is starting despite a poor order position, as we lack a superior alternative, but we remain cautious given his reliance on contact against Holmes. Additionally, while **Seiya Suzuki** (57.69) receives a slight bump for facing a slow-delivery pitcher, his overall efficiency remains marginal. We will be tracking the weather closely for the games involving **Josh Naylor** and **Cole Young**, as the current wind-out projections are pivotal to their success against Kay. Should any lineups shift significantly before lock, we are prepared to pivot, though the current configuration maintains our optimal balance of speed, platoon stability, and situational park factors.",
